DEEP[1]=('Chapter 15 is named Puruṣottama Yoga, the yoga of the Supreme Person. It is only 20 verses long, and is traditionally chanted before meals in many homes and monasteries, especially in South India and among ISKCON devotees.',
 'The Kaṭha Upaniṣad (2.3.1) says: <i>ūrdhva-mūlo’vāk-śākha eṣo’śvatthaḥ sanātanaḥ</i>. Śaṅkara explains <i>aśvattha</i> as “that which will not last until tomorrow” (<i>a-śvaḥ-stha</i>): the world is ever changing, though the process is endless.',
 'Our lives branch out in many directions. Remember where the roots are.')
DEEP[2]=('',
 'The verse is in triṣṭubh metre. The aśvattha is a fig, but commentators also use the banyan, whose aerial roots drop from branches and become new trunks. The secondary roots are <i>vāsanās</i>, latent desires and impressions, created by action. Śaṅkara says the higher branches are the worlds of gods, the lower ones animals. Rāmānuja says the human world is special, because here new karma is made.',
 'Each habit puts down a new root. Notice which roots you are feeding.')
DEEP[3]=('',
 'Commentators note that the tree cannot be understood from inside: we see branches and leaves, but not the whole. Yet it can be cut. <i>Asaṅga-śastra</i>, the weapon of non-attachment, is sharpened by discrimination and dispassion. Śaṅkara says the axe is detachment born of firm desire for the Supreme. Rāmānuja says detachment comes from knowing the faults of worldly pleasures.',
 'You don’t need to understand everything before you start letting go.')
DEEP[4]=('',
 'The words <i>tam eva cādyaṃ puruṣaṃ prapadye</i> are a prayer of surrender spoken inside the verse. Rāmānuja’s tradition values it as a model of <i>prapatti</i>. Commentators note the two movements: cutting (detachment) and turning (refuge). One without the other is incomplete. Detachment without a goal can become emptiness; devotion without detachment can become another attachment.',
 'Letting go is half the journey. Turning toward the good is the other half.')
DEEP[5]=('',
 'Commentators note that pride, <i>māna</i>, is named first, as humility, <i>amānitva</i>, was named first in 13.7. Śaṅkara says freedom from pride and delusion makes one fit for the goal. Rāmānuja says these are the qualities of one devoted to the Lord. The list gathers marks from earlier chapters: freedom from attachment (2.48), desire (2.71), and opposites (2.45).',
 'Humility and freedom from craving are simple marks of real progress. Watch for them in yourself.')
DEEP[6]=('',
 'Kaṭha 2.2.15, Muṇḍaka 2.2.10 and Śvetāśvatara 6.14 all say: <i>na tatra sūryo bhāti na candra-tārakam… tasya bhāsā sarvam idaṃ vibhāti</i>, “there the sun does not shine, nor moon nor stars… by its light all this shines”. Commentators note that the source of light needs no light. <i>Paramaṃ dhāma</i> recalls 8.21. Śaṅkara takes it as the state of Brahman; Rāmānuja and Madhva as the Lord’s abode.',
 'Some light needs no lamp. Awareness itself is that light.')
DEEP[7]=('',
 '<i>Aṃśa</i>, part or fragment, is central to the Vedānta debates. The Brahma Sūtra (2.3.43) says the soul is a part, <i>aṃśa</i>, of Brahman. Rāmānuja and Madhva use this to show the soul is eternally distinct. Śaṅkara reads it as a seeming part, like pot-space. Madhva calls the soul a <i>pratibimba</i>, reflection, of the Lord. All agree the soul is <i>sanātana</i>, eternal.',
 'You are a spark of the Divine. Remember your own dignity, and see it in others.')
DEEP[8]=('',
 '<i>Īśvara</i> here means the soul as master of the body. The image is gentle: the wind leaves the garden but carries its fragrance. Commentators explain the <i>sūkṣma śarīra</i>, subtle body, made of mind, intellect, senses and vital breaths. It carries the impressions of one life into the next. Śaṅkara and Rāmānuja agree on this picture.',
 'What we cultivate inside travels with us. Grow fragrant habits.')
DEEP[9]=('',
 'Commentators note the order of senses: hearing, sight, touch, taste and smell, linked to space, fire, air, water and earth. The soul presides over them, <i>adhiṣṭhāya</i>, as a rider over horses (the Kaṭha image). Śaṅkara says the soul seems to enjoy through them. Rāmānuja says the soul truly experiences, through the senses, according to its karma.',
 'Your senses are instruments. You are the one who experiences through them.')
DEEP[10]=('',
 'The <i>jñāna-cakṣus</i>, eye of knowledge, recalls 13.34. Commentators note that the soul is present in every experience, yet overlooked, as we see objects but rarely notice our own seeing. Śaṅkara says the deluded identify with the body and cannot see the soul. Rāmānuja says the wise see the self as distinct from the body even in daily life.',
 'Look beneath the surface of experience. There is always someone experiencing.')
DEEP[11]=('',
 '<i>Akṛtātman</i>, one whose mind is unprepared. Commentators stress that effort alone is not enough: the mind must be purified through right living, humility and understanding. Śaṅkara says those whose minds are not purified by austerity and self-control do not see, even with study. The verse warns against spiritual ambition without moral groundwork.',
 'Prepare the ground before planting. Effort on an unready mind bears little fruit.')
DEEP[12]=('',
 'Verses 12–15 show the Lord sustaining the world: as light (12), as gravity and nourishment (13), as digestion (14), and as the inner presence (15). Commentators link 15.12 to 7.8 and 10.21. Śaṅkara says the light in sun, moon and fire is the Lord’s, lent to them. Rāmānuja says the Lord gives each its power.',
 'Every light you see today can be a reminder of the light behind all lights.')
DEEP[13]=('',
 '<i>Soma</i> is both the moon and the sacred plant of the Vedic rites. Traditional belief held that moonlight nourishes plants and that herbs gathered on certain moon phases are stronger. Commentators say the Lord holds up the earth and gives sap to every plant. Śaṅkara takes <i>ojas</i> as the Lord’s power that keeps the earth from falling apart.',
 'Look at a plant today and remember who nourishes it. Gratitude grows from such small looks.')
DEEP[14]=('',
 'This verse is chanted before meals along with 4.24. <i>Vaiśvānara</i>, “belonging to all people”, is the fire in all beings; the Bṛhadāraṇyaka Upaniṣad (5.9.1) describes it as the fire within that digests food, heard as a sound when the ears are closed. The four kinds of food are <i>bhakṣya</i> (chewed), <i>bhojya</i> (swallowed), <i>lehya</i> (licked) and <i>coṣya</i> (sucked). Āyurveda calls digestion <i>agni</i>.',
 'Before eating, remember the fire within. Eat gratefully and moderately.')
DEEP[15]=('',
 '<i>Apohana</i>, loss or forgetting. Commentators read it in two ways: forgetting as mercy (we forget sorrows and past lives), and forgetting as the working of karma for the unrighteous. <i>Vedānta-kṛt</i>, maker of the Vedānta; some take this as Kṛṣṇa as Vyāsa, compiler of the Brahma Sūtras. Madhva cites this verse to show all scripture points to Viṣṇu. The verse is in triṣṭubh.',
 'The one you seek is already seated in your heart. Turn inward.')
DEEP[16]=('',
 'This verse introduces the threefold teaching of 15.16–18. Commentators differ on the meaning. Śaṅkara: <i>kṣara</i> is the changing world; <i>akṣara</i> is māyā, its unchanging seed. Rāmānuja: <i>kṣara</i> is bound souls in bodies; <i>akṣara</i> is freed souls. Madhva: <i>kṣara</i> is all souls; <i>akṣara</i> is Lakṣmī, the Lord’s eternal consort. All agree the third, beyond both, is the Lord.',
 'Notice what changes in you, and what does not. The unchanging part deserves your trust.')
DEEP[17]=('',
 '<i>Uttama puruṣa</i>, the highest Person, gives the chapter its name. Commentators note that this Person both enters the worlds (immanence) and stands beyond them (transcendence). Śaṅkara says the Supreme is the witness of all. Rāmānuja says the Supreme is the Lord distinct from bound and freed souls. Madhva says the Lord is distinct from all. The Taittirīya Upaniṣad (2.6) says, “having created it, he entered into it”.',
 'There is a support beneath all supports. Lean on it.')
DEEP[18]=('',
 '<i>Puruṣottama</i> is a name of Viṣṇu in the Viṣṇu Sahasranāma (name 24). The Jagannātha temple in Puri is called Puruṣottama Kṣetra. Vaiṣṇava traditions take this verse as a clear statement of the supremacy of the personal Lord. Śaṅkara reads Puruṣottama as Brahman beyond the world and its cause. Commentators note the Gītā’s typical method: a name explained by its meaning.',
 'The highest is not only a principle but a Person who can be loved. That makes devotion possible.')
DEEP[19]=('',
 '<i>Sarva-vit</i>, knower of all, and <i>sarva-bhāvena</i>, with the whole being: knowledge and devotion meet. Commentators of all schools see this verse as their meeting point. Śaṅkara says one who knows the Self as the Supreme knows everything worth knowing. Rāmānuja says such knowledge naturally becomes love. The Muṇḍaka question of 7.2 is answered here: knowing the Supreme, all is known.',
 'True knowing ends in love, and true love seeks to know. Let both grow together.')
DEEP[20]=('',
 '<i>Guhyatama</i>, most secret, recalls 9.1. <i>Kṛta-kṛtya</i>, “one who has done what is to be done”, is a phrase for one whose life’s purpose is fulfilled. Commentators call chapter 15 the essence of Vedānta in 20 verses. Śaṅkara says knowing this, nothing more remains to be done. Rāmānuja says the devotee who knows this has attained life’s goal. The colophon names chapter 15 Puruṣottama Yoga.',
 'A short chapter can hold a whole philosophy. Learn this one by heart if you can.')
